CAT = "llqpAnnuities"; SEG = "ANN"
Q = [
("A life annuity is designed mainly to protect against the risk of:",
 "Outliving one's savings", ["Dying too soon in retirement", "Disability before age 65", "Rising interest on debts"],
 "A life annuity pays for life however long the annuitant lives, so it transfers longevity risk to the insurer."),
("A retiree wants payments for life but worries about dying soon after buying an annuity. Which option fits best?",
 "A life annuity with a guarantee", ["A straight life annuity", "A 5-year term certain annuity", "A deferred annuity starting at age 90"],
 "A guarantee period (for example 10 years) continues payments to the beneficiary if the annuitant dies early, at a slightly lower income than straight life."),
("A joint and last survivor annuity pays:",
 "Until the second annuitant dies", ["Until the first annuitant dies", "For a fixed 20 years only", "A lump sum paid at the second death"],
 "Payments continue as long as either annuitant is alive, often at a reduced rate after the first death if chosen."),
("Of these annuities bought with the same amount by a 65-year-old, which pays the highest monthly income?",
 "A straight life annuity", ["A life annuity with 15 years guaranteed", "A joint and last survivor annuity", "An indexed life annuity"],
 "Straight life has no refund or guarantee and covers one life, so it gives the most income per dollar. Each added feature lowers the payment."),
("A prescribed annuity bought with non-registered money is attractive because:",
 "Taxable interest is level each year", ["All payments are tax-free", "It defers all tax to death", "It can only be bought inside an RRSP"],
 "A prescribed annuity spreads the interest portion evenly over its term, so the taxable amount is the same each year instead of being higher early."),
("Payments from an annuity bought with RRSP money are:",
 "Fully taxable", ["Taxable only on the interest", "Tax-free", "Taxed as capital gains"],
 "Registered money has never been taxed, so every payment from a registered annuity is income. Only the interest portion is taxed on non-registered annuities."),
("An impaired (enhanced) annuity pays more than a standard one because:",
 "The annuitant is expected to die sooner", ["The annuitant pays a higher premium for it", "It has a longer guarantee period", "It is held inside a TFSA"],
 "A person with a serious health condition is expected to receive fewer payments, so the insurer can pay a higher income for the same premium."),
("An insured annuity strategy combines a life annuity with:",
 "Permanent life cover to replace capital", ["A term policy for 10 years only", "A critical illness policy", "A segregated fund with a reset feature"],
 "The annuity gives high, secure income; part of it pays for permanent life insurance that replaces the capital at death for the heirs."),
("An RRSP must be converted to an income option by the end of the year the annuitant turns:",
 "71", ["65", "69", "75"],
 "By December 31 of the year the holder turns 71, the RRSP must be collapsed, used to buy an annuity or transferred to a RRIF."),
("What is the main difference between an RRSP and a RRIF?",
 "A RRIF has yearly minimum withdrawals", ["A RRIF accepts new contributions", "An RRSP pays tax-free income", "An RRSP must be bought from an insurer"],
 "A RRIF is a payout vehicle with a minimum withdrawal each year after the first; RRSP contributions are not allowed into it."),
("Ines contributed to a spousal RRSP this year and her husband withdraws from it the next year. The withdrawal is taxed:",
 "To Ines", ["To her husband", "Half to each spouse", "At a flat 10%"],
 "Under the attribution rule, a withdrawal in the year of any spousal contribution or the next two calendar years is taxed to the contributing spouse."),
("At death, a RRIF can pass to a surviving spouse without immediate tax if the spouse is named as:",
 "Successor annuitant", ["Executor of the will", "Trustee for the children", "Power of attorney"],
 "Naming the spouse or common-law partner as successor annuitant (or beneficiary with a transfer) defers the tax that would otherwise be due on the full value in the final return."),
("Withdrawals from a TFSA are:",
 "Tax-free; room comes back next year", ["Taxed as regular income", "Taxed only if over age 71", "Penalized if they are taken before age 60"],
 "TFSA withdrawals are not taxed, and the amount withdrawn is added back to contribution room on January 1 of the following year."),
("A worker leaves an employer and moves her pension's commuted value to a locked-in account. That account is a:",
 "LIRA", ["TFSA", "RESP", "RDSP"],
 "A locked-in retirement account holds pension money that must stay locked in for retirement income, later moved to a LIF or used for an annuity. Unlocking rules vary by jurisdiction."),
("Educational assistance payments from an RESP are taxed:",
 "To the student", ["To the subscriber", "To no one", "To the school"],
 "EAPs (growth and government grants) are taxed in the student's hands, usually at a low rate. The subscriber's contributions can come back tax-free."),
("A deferred annuity differs from an immediate annuity in that payments:",
 "Start at a later date", ["Are never taxed", "Stop at the first missed premium", "Are paid as one lump sum"],
 "An immediate annuity starts paying within a year of purchase; a deferred annuity builds value first and starts income later."),
]
